baseline gamma=1 (shipped rule)    11.56t    44.6%    3.79b    38.0%   24.6%     1.6%     0.0%     26.3%     15.43
M2  direction-blind (charge all)   15.78t   100.0%   21.48b     0.0%    0.0%     0.0%     0.0%     67.9%     64.08
M2' closed form, NO watermarks     11.56t    44.6%    3.79b     0.0%   24.6%     1.6%     0.0%     26.3%     15.43

  M2 must diverge wildly - it proves the extension/retracement DIRECTION logic is
  what produces the separation, and is not decoration.
    PASS

  M2' must be IDENTICAL to the baseline.  If it is, then the per-side extension
  budget is an IDENTITY, not a constraint: on the up side the unspent budget always
  equals (tick - T0) exactly, because every up-move credits it and every down-move
  above T0 debits it.  Consequence for the build, if it holds:
      d0 = tick_before - T0 ; d1 = tick_after - T0
      charged = (d0*d1 >= 0) ? max(0, |d0| - |d1|) : |d0|
  ONE storage slot (T0).  The intra-block high/low WATERMARKS OF 2.1 ARE REDUNDANT,
  and '2.4c: capped by the extension' is a tautology, not a separate defence.
    PASS - identical, so the identity holds and the watermarks are dead weight.

  M3 - the 2.4c dust-poison test, run directly rather than inferred:
    poison extension 0.250 ticks ; the $200k opposite swap moves 199.0 ticks and is charged for 0.250 of them ($4.95).
    PASS - poisoning authorises only its own extension.
